1. INTRODUCTION & OVERVIEW
Ajali! (Swahili for "accident" or "incident") is a full-stack emergency incident reporting platform. It allows members of the public to report accidents, hazards, and emergencies with a title, description, category, and precise geolocation, optionally attaching photos or videos as evidence. Reports move through a defined status lifecycle (Reported → Under Review → In Progress → Resolved/Rejected) that is fully auditable, and administrators have a dedicated back-office interface to triage, filter, and bulk-manage incoming reports.
The system is split into two independently deployable codebases that communicate exclusively over a JSON REST API:
•	A Django REST Framework backend ("ajali-web-app-backend") that owns all business logic, data persistence, authentication, and third-party integrations (file storage, geocoding, email).
•	A React single-page application ("ajali-app-frontend"), built with Vite and state-managed with Redux Toolkit, that consumes the backend API and renders the public-facing and admin interfaces.
1.1 Core Features
•	Email/password registration and login with JWT-based session management and automatic access-token refresh.
•	Incident reporting with geolocation (latitude/longitude + human-readable address), category/type tagging, and an option to report anonymously.
•	Photo and video evidence attachment via Cloudinary, with signed, direct-to-cloud client uploads.
•	Forward and reverse geocoding (address ⇄ coordinates) to simplify location entry.
•	A full, timestamped status-change audit trail per incident (who changed the status, from what, to what, and why).
•	A role-based admin console for reviewing, searching, filtering, and bulk-updating incident statuses, plus user role management and an admin action audit log.
•	An in-app notification system with per-user preferences (email/SMS/push toggles), unread counts, and bulk read/delete actions.
1.2 Intended Users
•	Members of the public ("user" role) — report incidents, track their own reports, and manage their notifications.
•	Administrators ("admin" role) — review all incidents platform-wide, update statuses, manage user accounts and roles, and audit admin activity.
 
2. SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE
The platform follows a decoupled client-server architecture. The React frontend never touches the database directly — every read or write goes through the versioned REST API exposed by the Django backend.
React SPA (Vite)  ──axios + JWT──▶  Django REST API  ──▶  PostgreSQL
      │                                    │
      │                                    ├──▶ Cloudinary (media storage)
 Redux Toolkit                              ├──▶ Nominatim / OpenStreetMap (geocoding)
 (client state)                             └──▶ SMTP (password-reset emails)
2.1 Request Flow Example — Reporting an Incident
•	User fills the "Create Incident" form in the React app; React Hook Form + Yup validate the fields client-side.
•	The frontend optionally calls the reverse/forward geocoding endpoints to convert a map pin or typed address into coordinates.
•	axiosInstance attaches the JWT access token from localStorage to the Authorization header and POSTs to /api/incidents/.
•	Django's JWTAuthentication class validates the token and identifies the requesting user; the IncidentListCreateView creates the Incident row and its first IncidentStatusHistory entry (status = "reported") inside a single database transaction.
•	If the user attaches media, the frontend requests a signed Cloudinary upload signature from the backend, uploads the file directly to Cloudinary from the browser, then registers the resulting media URL against the incident via the image/video upload endpoints.
•	The created incident (including nested media and status history) is returned as JSON and added to the Redux incident slice, which re-renders the UI.
2.2 Why This Architecture
•	Decoupling frontend and backend allows either to be redeployed, scaled, or replaced independently (e.g. a future mobile app could reuse the same API).
•	JWT authentication is stateless, so the API can scale horizontally without a shared session store.
•	Direct-to-Cloudinary uploads (via signed signatures) avoid routing large image/video payloads through the Django server, reducing backend load and upload latency.
•	Business logic for status transitions and incident creation is centralized in service functions (apps/incidents/services.py), not duplicated across views, so both the user-facing and admin endpoints share identical, auditable behaviour.
 
