6. AUTHENTICATION & AUTHORIZATION
6.1 JWT Token Flow
The API uses JSON Web Tokens via djangorestframework-simplejwt. On successful login or registration+login, the server issues two tokens:
Token	Lifetime	Purpose
access_token	1 hour	Sent as "Authorization: Bearer <token>" on every authenticated request
refresh_token	30 days	Exchanged for a new access token via /api/auth/refresh/ once the access token expires
On the frontend, axios request/response interceptors (src/api/axiosConfig.js) automate this: every outgoing request automatically attaches the stored access token, and if a request comes back with HTTP 401, the interceptor silently calls /api/auth/refresh/, stores the new access token, and retries the original request once. If the refresh itself fails, the user is redirected to /login.
6.2 Roles & Permission Classes
Permission Class	Effect
permissions.IsAuthenticated (DRF built-in)	Requires a valid access token; used on nearly all non-public endpoints
permissions.AllowAny (DRF built-in)	No authentication required (registration, login, password reset, public incident feed)
core.permissions.IsAdminUser	Requires an authenticated user with role == "admin"; used on every /api/admin/ endpoint
core.permissions.IsOwnerOrAdmin	Object-level: allows the resource's owner OR any admin
apps.incidents.permissions.IsIncidentOwner	Object-level: only the user who reported the incident may view/edit/delete it or its media
Note on IncidentDetailView: the queryset intentionally is NOT pre-filtered by request.user. This is a deliberate design choice — if it were filtered, a non-owner requesting someone else's incident would get a 404 ("not found"), which is ambiguous. Instead, the queryset returns the row and IsIncidentOwner.has_object_permission() explicitly rejects non-owners with a 403 ("forbidden"), giving a clearer, more correct HTTP semantic.
6.3 Password Requirements
•	Minimum 8 characters
•	At least one uppercase letter, one lowercase letter, one digit, and one special character
•	Enforced server-side by validate_password_strength() on both registration and password change/reset — client-side validation is a UX convenience only, not a security boundary
 
