8. STANDARD RESPONSE & ERROR FORMATS
8.1 Errors
A custom DRF exception handler (core/exceptions.py) wraps every non-2xx response in a consistent envelope:
{
  "error": true,
  "message": "<short description>",
  "details": { ... },      // the original DRF error payload (field-level errors, etc.)
  "status_code": 400
}
Some hand-written views (e.g. login, change-password) return a simpler {"error": "..."} shape directly rather than going through this wrapper — when integrating, handle both shapes defensively.
8.2 Pagination
Endpoints using CustomPagination (currently the notifications list) return:
{
  "count": 34,
  "next": "http://.../?page=2",
  "previous": null,
  "page": 1,
  "total_pages": 2,
  "results": [ ... ]
}
Most other list endpoints (incidents, users, admin lists) currently return a plain JSON array with no pagination envelope, even though DRF's global DEFAULT_PAGINATION_CLASS is configured — worth confirming before assuming pagination behaviour on any given list endpoint.
 
9. FILE UPLOADS (CLOUDINARY)
Incident photos and videos are not uploaded through the Django backend. Instead, the platform uses Cloudinary's signed direct upload pattern:
1.	The client requests a signature from GET /api/incidents/{id}/upload-signature/, scoped to that incident's folder.
2.	The client uploads the raw file straight to Cloudinary's API using that signature — the file bytes never touch the Django server.
3.	Cloudinary returns a secure URL and public_id, which the client then registers against the incident via POST /api/incidents/{id}/images/ or /videos/.
4.	The backend independently validates the declared mime_type and file_size_bytes against an allow-list (images: jpg/jpeg/png/webp up to 10 MB; videos: mp4/mov/webm up to 50 MB) before saving the IncidentMedia record.
Deleting media (DELETE .../images/{media_id}/ or .../videos/{media_id}/) currently removes the IncidentMedia database row; core/cloudinary_utils.py also exposes a delete_from_cloudinary(public_id) helper for removing the underlying asset from Cloudinary storage itself.
 
