11. KNOWN LIMITATIONS & NOTES
These are implementation details worth being aware of when extending or defending this project:
•	The password-reset endpoint (/api/auth/forgot-password/) currently returns the raw reset_token in the JSON response, in addition to emailing it. This is convenient for local testing but should be removed before any production deployment, since it bypasses the email verification step entirely.
•	List endpoints are inconsistent in whether they return a paginated envelope or a plain array — the notifications list uses CustomPagination, while the incidents and admin list endpoints currently return a plain JSON array despite DRF's global pagination default being configured.
•	Deleting a user (via /api/auth/users/{id}/ or /api/admin/users/{id}/) is a soft delete (is_active = False), not a permanent removal — the account and its historical incidents remain in the database, which is intentional for audit/history integrity.
•	The IncidentDetailView queryset is deliberately unfiltered by owner so that permission checks return 403 (not 404) for non-owners — an important design decision to understand if extending this view.
•	Admin bulk status updates silently skip any incident that is already at the target status, rather than erroring, so "updated_count" in the response may be lower than the number of IDs submitted.
•	Geocoding relies on the free, rate-limited Nominatim/OpenStreetMap service — high-traffic production use would need a paid geocoding provider or caching layer.
 
